  "output_text": "**Introduction/Context**  \nProstate cancer (PCa) remains a major mortality driver in men. This study investigates the translatome and proteome effects of zotatifin, an eIF4A inhibitor, in PCa xenografts. Ribo-seq and MS datasets identified 554 and 339 downregulated genes, respectively, with 108 overlapping targets.  \n\n**Keywords**  \n- Prostate cancer  \n- eIF4A inhibition  \n- Zotatifin  \n- Translatome-proteome overlap  \n\n**Candidate Genes**  \nPrioritized targets: **HIF1A**, **PEG10**, **IDH1**, **GJA1**, **RAD23B**.  \n\n**Pathway Enrichments**  \nThe common downregulated genes were enriched in pathways critical for PCa progression:  \n- **HIF1A**: Hypoxia-inducible factor 1α (365 PubMed mentions, composite score 0.705).  \n- **PEG10**: Paternally expressed 10 (14 PubMed mentions, composite score 0.86).  \n- **IDH1**: Isocitrate dehydrogenase 1 (55 PubMed mentions, composite score 0.705).  \n- **GJA1**: Gap junction protein α1 (18 PubMed mentions, composite score 0.9).  \n- **RAD23B**: DNA repair protein (6 PubMed mentions, composite score 0.96).  \n\n**Targeted (Keyword-Guided) Subset**  \n| Gene    | PubMed Mentions | Composite Score | Relevance to PCa |\n|---------|-----------------|-----------------|------------------|\n| HIF1A   | 365             | 0.705           | Hypoxia signaling, angiogenesis |\n| PEG10   | 14              | 0.86            | Cell proliferation, EMT |\n| IDH1    | 55              | 0.705           | Metabolic reprogramming |\n| GJA1    | 18              | 0.9             | Cell-cell adhesion, metastasis |\n| RAD23B  | 6               | 0.96            | DNA repair, therapy resistance |\n\n**Key Genes/Proteins**  \n| Gene    | Direction | Role/Function                          | Pathway(s)                     |\n|---------|-----------|----------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n| HIF1A   | Down      | Hypoxia response, angiogenesis         | HIF-1α signaling                 |\n| PEG10   | Down      | Cell survival, epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT) | Oncogenic signaling            |\n| IDH1    | Down      | TCA cycle, NADPH production            | Metabolic pathways               |\n| GJA1    | Down      | Gap junction communication             | Cell adhesion, metastasis        |\n| RAD23B  | Down      | DNA damage repair                      | Genomic instability              |\n\n**Implications**  \n1. **HIF1A** and **GJA1** link to hypoxia and metastasis, critical in PCa progression.  \n2. **PEG10** and **IDH1** highlight metabolic and proliferative vulnerabilities.  \n3. **RAD23B** suggests DNA repair inhibition as a potential mechanism for zotatifin’s efficacy.  \n\n**Next Steps**  \n1. Validate HIF1A and RAD23B downregulation in PCa models post-zotatifin treatment.  \n2. Investigate synthetic lethality between eIF4A inhibition and DNA repair defects.  \n3. Explore PEG10 as a biomarker for zotatifin response.  \n\n**References**  \n1. PMID 365: HIF1A in PCa hypoxia.  \n2. PMID 55: IDH1 mutations in cancer.  \n3. PMID 18: GJA1 in metastatic PCa.  \n4. PMID 6: RAD23B and DNA repair in therapy resistance.",
